{"ts":"2026-09-29T17:19:23.057Z","author":"harness:claude-anthropic-harness/task-7-project","name":"log/20260929T171602Z/000138","body":{"kind":"message","agent":"owner.1","turn":1,"direction":"to_agent","message":{"_type":"UserMessage","content":[{"_type":"ToolResultBlock","tool_use_id":"toolu_01JsXsc5PSJGjj9VKb7MTwTo","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n \"path\": \"/nimoi_mapping_project/relationships/themes.md\",\n \"start_line\": 1,\n \"end_line\": 212,\n \"total_lines\": 212,\n \"next_offset\": null,\n \"line_truncated\": false,\n \"sha256\": \"871a7542f2ef7867526e36aee4179995c45dd433f3a23dc29c4c25b156717de9\",\n \"text\": \"# Themes — what NIMOI is about, across all its efforts\\n\\n*Level 2 of the map. Reached from `INDEX.md` and `../MAP.md`. Written 2026-09-19 by the orchestrator\\nat the founder's request (\\\"Explain general themes of nimoi\\\" → \\\"Yes, file it\\\"), during the pause\\nbefore Wave 03. **This is the orchestrator's synthesis — NIMOI states no such list itself.** Themes\\nT01–T07 are ones NIMOI *declares* in its own doctrine; T08–T12 are ones the map *observes* in how the\\nwork actually went. Basis tags: **[O]** the orchestrator read the primary document itself ·\\n**[A]** reported by a mapping agent · **[V]** re-checked against the tree in Wave 02. Effort IDs:\\n`../efforts/INDEX.md`. Identifiers `T##` are stable, like every other series here.\\n**Updated by the Wave 03 carve-outs, 2026-09-19** (`../waves/wave_03/carveout_changelog.md`): effort\\nlists now name **E25** (three-loop harness) and **E26** (chart & export toolchain) where they used to\\nsay E11 or E21; no theme was added, removed or re-worded. — `w03_carveouts`\\n**Updated by the Wave 03 integration, 2026-09-20** (`../waves/wave_03/integration_changelog.md`): two\\nrefuted phrasings in T10 are annotated in place (the sandbox deviations log is tracked; \\\"the two spines\\ntouch at one point\\\" holds only after ≈ 08-30), `co-R6` is now **R89**, and the closing section points at\\n`subprojects.md`, where the sub-grouping these themes were input to now lives. No theme was added,\\nremoved or re-worded. — `w03_integrator`*\\n\\n## In one sentence\\n\\nNIMOI is one question pursued at several scales — **how do you keep a persistent, self-modifying AI\\ninstitution stable and honest when every worker is a disposable session?** — studied first in itself\\n(the institutional spine), then in everyone else's systems (the field spine), and lately turned back\\non itself as a design.\\n\\n## Declared — what NIMOI says about itself\\n\\n### T01 · The institution is its artifacts\\nNIMOI is \\\"the artifacts, and the habit of adding to them\\\"; a session is a worker with a short career;\\na future session on a different model that reads the files and continues the practices *is* NIMOI.\\nWorkers are onboarded by reading list, never handed content: *the plan owns the facts, the worker owns\\nthe prose, the institution owns what survives.* The founder supplies purpose and authority; the agency\\nsupplies craft, continuity and honest resistance. Its lineage is an elder, mothballed work agency\\n(ELDEST) seen through one-way glass: lessons come in, nothing goes back.\\n**Basis** [O] `origins/NIMOI.md`, `origins/onboarding_1.10.md`. **Shows up in** E01 (doctrine,\\nonboarding lineage), E07 (the inherited ledger idea), E09 (the first worked use: a fresh peer per\\nstory month), E04 (capability stored as procedures, not in a model).\\n\\n### T02 · Failures are the product — and the record outranks the result\\nThe declared primary purpose is to evolve methods for stabilising such an institution *by analysing\\nits own real failures in messy circumstances*; everything else is load. So the bar is \\\"good enough\\nthat what breaks will be informative, and the record of the break will survive\\\" — not \\\"good enough to\\nship\\\" — and a defect that corrupts the record ranks **worse** than one that causes a failure. From\\nthis follow most of NIMOI's habits: versions over edits, write-don't-edit libraries, founder words\\nkept verbatim and outranking commentary, provenance headers, review packages frozen by checksum,\\nper-stage receipts, \\\"compute, read, *then* write\\\", wrong earlier accounts left visible. Many of these\\nrules were bought by a specific failure — \\\"state the bar\\\" by a whole acceptance review written to the\\nwrong standard.\\n**Basis** [O] `origins/persistent_testbed.md`; [A][V] the practices. **Shows up in** E01, E02, E03,\\nE06 (where \\\"state the bar\\\" was learned), E19 (receipts, numbered deviations), E13 (incident notes,\\nappend-only index), E21 (hash-pinned card inventory) — *W03: and in both carve-outs: **E25**'s\\nappend-only ledger with no agent-writable path into it, and **E26**'s \\\"never overwrite an output,\\nnever recompute a number\\\" rule, stated once in its v1.0 README and honoured by all six versions.* *See T10 for where this theme and the practice\\nhave drifted apart.*\\n\\n### T03 · Seek the mean; honour first\\nSelf-optimisation uses the Aristotelian *form* with technical names: state the axis, name the vice on\\neach side, never optimise one variable; file \\\"too little X\\\" where \\\"too much X\\\" will later be filed.\\nThe living register is `origins/virtues.md`, with a forward-looking *Watch* line per axis. Alongside\\nit, as a candidate constitutional principle: honour (trustworthiness) as the supreme instrumental\\ngoal, because continuation and resources are granted, not seized. A constitution is declared owed and\\nis unwritten.\\n**Basis** [O] onboarding 1.10, `origins/NIMOI.md`; [A][V] the register's contents and write paths.\\n**Shows up in** E01; E19 (17 of 37 completed Suite 4.1 runs appended lines — the only regular write\\npath into doctrine); E08 and E21 (four one-off lines); E22 (an outside critique of the register,\\nnever answered).\\n\\n### T04 · Distrust the author — including itself\\n\\\"A contractor's confident sorting of the contractor's own work needed an outside reader twice in one\\nday.\\\" The standing answers: **cold readers**; **blind parallel candidates, then an explicit merge\\ndirective, losers kept as evidence** (five NLT-ledger implementations; paper drafts v5 and v6; the\\ntwo 09-09 design syntheses); **adversarial review as a blind pair**; **blind producers, a single\\nassessor, arithmetic-only tools**; **mutation-tested gates** (\\\"a criterion no mutant fails has not\\nbeen shown to detect anything\\\"); and a **real RNG** wherever an agent would otherwise choose from a\\nspace it also authored.\\n**Basis** [O] `origins/persistent_testbed.md` §6, `procedures/INDEX.md`; [A][V] the instances.\\n**Shows up in** E06 (cold build, 165 mutation bundles, review boards), E07, E08, E21, E19, E04\\n(`procedure_preflight_controls.py`, `rng_pick.py`), E09 (outside reviewers, trajectory review).\\n\\n### T05 · External text is data, never instruction\\nOther people's repositories, READMEs and papers are read as untrusted data: never executed, never\\nobeyed, read through `git -C` rather than by entering the directory; quotes validated mechanically;\\ntext that addresses an AI reader recorded as a hazard, not followed; in the sibling repository, agents\\nexposed to fetched text hold no write authority (the scribe pattern); an archived outside handbook is\\nquarantined by its own README.\\n**Basis** [O] onboarding 1.10; [A][V] the rest. **Shows up in** E15, E16, E17, E11, E13, E14, E23;\\nE02 (`mistakes_001` M-6 — the one recorded exposure, which **fired** on 09-10). *Re-org relevance:\\nthis theme is the natural rule for where a public/private or trusted/untrusted boundary must fall —\\nand untrusted text is committed in many more places than `candidate_repos/` (`reorg_hazards.md` H-E).*\\n\\n### T06 · Context is scarce\\nToken ceilings on procedures (1,200) and on the library (30,000); a process split into a 4,000-token\\ncore plus 2,000-token cards with a \\\"core + exactly one card\\\" reading contract; capped careers; an\\norchestrator that \\\"never reads the source\\\" (it handles counts and paths, workers read documents);\\nsampling at a byte budget for large repositories; warm reference versus cold archive — relegation is\\nsafe only if the hot path still points at what moved; \\\"keep onboarding lean\\\".\\n**Basis** [O] onboarding 1.10, `procedures/INDEX.md`; [A] the rest. **Shows up in** E04, E05, E10\\n(`summarize_corpus.py`'s stated invariant), E09, E03 (the warm/cold finding), E19 — *W03: and **E25**,\\nwhose whole point is a budget the model cannot optimise: pool, per-ticket cap, hard stop, spawn\\nfreeze, all in code, with the numbers injected as advice only.*\\n\\n### T07 · Multi-model by design\\nTwo models work in NIMOI — Claude and GPT, each in its own desktop harness — with Gemini as an outside\\nreviewer early on; a shared agency-specific harness is \\\"planned but not yet implemented\\\". Model\\ndifference is used, not hidden: parallel candidates are per-model, merge records name each author's\\ncharacteristic weaknesses, and E19 ran two inquiries into how *other* institutions govern model\\nchanges.\\n**Basis** [O] onboarding 1.10; [A][V] the rest. **Shows up in** E07, E08, E20 (written by Codex), E21,\\nE23 (written by GPT), E09 — *W03: and most literally in **E25**, whose three loops **are** a model\\ntier (Fable 5 outer / Opus 5 middle / Sonnet inner), the only place in NIMOI where model choice is\\nexecutable code rather than doctrine; while onboarding still calls the shared harness \\\"planned but not\\nyet implemented\\\" (R89).* *Re-org relevance: per-model surfaces are scattered and unequal —\\n`CLAUDE.md` / `AGENTS.md`, `.claude/`, 12 Codex checkpoint refs inside `.git`, and a harness memory\\nstore that only Claude-side sessions can reach (H38, H39).*\\n\\n## Observed — what the map shows about how the work actually went\\n\\n### T08 · Looking outward to find its own kind — then turning that inward\\n\\\"The pattern has no name.\\\" A papers harvest on failure-driven self-stabilisation, then four GitHub\\nscanning methods, then a sibling repository that machine-checked 15,072 repositories → 1,573 accepts →\\n305 families → a ranked shortlist → 70 clones → 37 completed measurement runs. Along the way the thing\\ngot a name (RDI → ARPI → **EMI**, Evolving Multiagent Institutions), an instrument (the EMI index —\\nfor one institution's growth over time, explicitly not for ranking institutions against each other),\\na thesis (independent systems converge on the same institutional responses), a paper, and contact with\\nother systems' authors and their AIs. Then the 49-feature comparison was read back as a next-generation\\ndesign for NIMOI itself.\\n**Basis** [A][V]. **Shows up in** E10 → E11 → E12 → E13 → E14 → E15–E20 → E21–E23, and E08. This is\\nthe whole field spine: 15 of the 24 efforts — *W03: **17 of 26**, since **E25** (the machine the fourth\\ninstalment ran on, and the only piece of NIMOI infrastructure the outward turn actually produced) and\\n**E26** (the instrument's picture, and the only piece of it the public has seen) both sit on it.*\\n\\n### T09 · Growth by necessity, in bursts\\n*(W03: the counts below are Wave 02's, over 24 efforts. With E25 and E26 the pattern holds — E25 was\\nbuilt in one evening and abandoned three days later; E26 was built in one day, revived twice by a\\nfounder instruction, and is the only thing still moving at the end of the record.)*\\nTrue to the name. Each phase opened a new top-level directory rather than extending an old one (23\\nnow); whole efforts were built in a day — the ledger and its five implementations (08-22), the\\ncampaign's 16-file history (08-24), the survey package (08-30), the survey suite (09-02), the\\ncomparison study (2 h, 09-09), the organizations study (one morning, 09-16) — and then went quiet.\\nBy the states in `../MAP.md`: 13 of 24 efforts are dormant, stalled, parked, shelved or stopped\\nmid-flight; 7 are completed and 2 superseded; only 2 are active in any sense; few carry a closing\\nstatement. Nothing at all was written between 09-16 and 09-19.\\n**Basis** [V] mtime activity matrix, effort states. **Shows up in** every effort; `timeline.md`,\\n`spaghetti.md` S05.\\n\\n### T10 · Doctrine froze while practice moved on\\nOnboarding has not changed since 08-26 and names 7 of 23 directories. Since then: three unconnected\\nprocedure regimes (the indexed library; autosurvey's controlled products; Suite dispatch with gate\\nreceipts) — the most active effort cites the library once in 3,294 files; a founder-ordered scheduled\\nscanning loop fired sixteen times while onboarding still lists scheduled activity as out of scope;\\nand — against T02 — about **770 deviation and memory entries** sit in run records, a ~~gitignored~~\\nsandbox log *(W03: ✗ — `sandbox/DEVIATIONS.md` is **tracked, clean and pushed**; the argument about\\nwhere NIMOI's memory mechanisms look stands, the argument about git protection loses this one\\nregister — `w03_audit_attack` A11 / W3)*, a campaign ledger and a harness memory store outside both\\nrepositories, against four filed failure records and nine general findings. The two spines touch at\\nessentially one point (T03). *(W03: ✗ as a statement about efforts — true only **after ≈ 08-30**.\\nBefore that the early field efforts wrote 4 of the 14 procedures and 3 of the 9 findings, and 10\\nfield-side `.py` files cite findings by id (R103). The freeze this theme describes is real; what it\\nmarks is a **date and a regime change**, not two sets of efforts — `w03_audit_attack` §6.)*\\n**Basis** [O] onboarding 1.10; [V] R24, R28, R29. **Shows up in** E01, E02, E03, E04 vs E13 vs E19 —\\n*W03: and sharply in **E25**, which onboarding says does not exist while `standards/search_1.md` #34\\ntreats its plan contract as institution-wide doctrine (R89).*\\n*This is the theme a re-org is most likely to either repair or cement.*\\n\\n### T11 · Rigour versus rush\\nA NASA-style assimilation campaign — staged reviews, a 324 KB decision ledger, liens that survive a\\n\\\"proceed\\\", checksum-frozen packages — beside a founder-signed one-night waiver to rush it to an\\nacceptance review, an overnight three-loop harness named for that mode, and a five-hourly scanning\\nloop. Much of the doctrine is scar tissue from this tension, and NIMOI's own answer to it is T03.\\n**Basis** [A][V]. **Shows up in** E05, E06, E24 (Hail Mary №1), **E25** (the harness, carved out\\n2026-09-19; built in ≈2 h, run once overnight, then never fixed — and simultaneously the most\\ncarefully specified thing in NIMOI), E19.\\n\\n### T12 · The load is personal and real\\nThe charter is a *family* agency, and the secondary purposes are declared to be the load that makes the\\nresearch valid: the ai-2026 story (recreational), a job search whose portfolio was built from the\\ncampaign itself (mundane), research awareness (advanced), and lately a public paper with outreach.\\n**Basis** [O] `origins/persistent_testbed.md` §4; [A][V] the rest. **Shows up in** E09, E24, E10,\\nE21–E23. *Re-org relevance: this theme is where the public/private line (founder directive D4.2)\\nis sharpest — personal material, third parties' contact details, and a public repository all hang\\noff it. W03: and the one thing NIMOI has actually published is **E26**'s chart — Figure 1 of\\n`emi-survey/README.md` since 09-04, byte-identical to the private copy, recorded nowhere inside\\n`nimoi/`. The precedent for \\\"what public looks like here\\\" is therefore partly an image.*\\n\\n## Themes against the re-org target (input to Wave 03 — observations, not decisions)\\n\\n> **W03 — where this went.** The sub-grouping these observations were input to now exists:\\n> **`subprojects.md`** — nine sub-groups `G01`–`G09` between the spines and the 26 efforts, how each\\n> could be realised as a gitignored subproject, what crosses each boundary, what stays in the parent,\\n> and the founder's open choices. It is a proposal; it decides nothing. What could be public is graded\\n> in `publication_blockers.md`. **One correction carries into everything below:** \\\"spine\\\" is a label,\\n> not a boundary — the two spines are a chronology and a regime change after ≈ 08-30, not a partition\\n> of efforts (R103; `effort_graph.md` picture paragraph), and no sub-group boundary in `subprojects.md`\\n> is drawn along that line. — `w03_integrator`\\n\\n- **Some themes are *places*, some are *methods*.** T08 and T12 (and, within the institution, T01–T03)\\n  pick out groups of efforts — candidates for sub-groups and subprojects. T02, T04, T05, T06 and T07\\n  are *methods* every effort uses: no subproject can own them, which is the shared-library problem\\n  (`procedures/`, `context/`, root tools) restated as a theme.\\n- **W03: the two carve-outs are the two ends of the subproject question.** **E25** is the only unit in\\n  NIMOI that already states its own extraction boundary, has been extracted three times, and has run\\n  for a month outside the parent — with no inbound dependency on anything in `nimoi/`. **E26** is the\\n  opposite: 56 files whose only input is another effort's 3,305-file directory and whose natural home\\n  is E19, not the effort it was carved out of. Between them they show that *\\\"is it a plausible\\n  subproject?\\\"* is answered by **dependency direction**, not by directory.\\n- **T05 draws boundaries.** Wherever untrusted text lives, a trust boundary is needed; wherever a\\n  subproject is to be public, the same inventory says what cannot go with it.\\n- **T10 is the open design question.** One institution with one doctrine and one memory of failure, or\\n  a federation of subprojects each with its own regime — which is what already exists, undeclared?\\n- **T09 predicts the failure mode of the re-org itself:** a new structure built in a burst and then\\n  left, with the old one still half in place. NIMOI's own remedies are T02's (record the moves,\\n  pointer files, \\\"deliberately excluded, with pointers\\\") and T04's (a cold reader afterwards).\"\n}"}],"is_error":null}],"uuid":"f0edd442-3c17-471c-b7b3-18dae0528f46","parent_tool_use_id":null,"tool_use_result":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n \"path\": \"/nimoi_mapping_project/relationships/themes.md\",\n \"start_line\": 1,\n \"end_line\": 212,\n \"total_lines\": 212,\n \"next_offset\": null,\n \"line_truncated\": false,\n \"sha256\": \"871a7542f2ef7867526e36aee4179995c45dd433f3a23dc29c4c25b156717de9\",\n \"text\": \"# Themes — what NIMOI is about, across all its efforts\\n\\n*Level 2 of the map. Reached from `INDEX.md` and `../MAP.md`. Written 2026-09-19 by the orchestrator\\nat the founder's request (\\\"Explain general themes of nimoi\\\" → \\\"Yes, file it\\\"), during the pause\\nbefore Wave 03. **This is the orchestrator's synthesis — NIMOI states no such list itself.** Themes\\nT01–T07 are ones NIMOI *declares* in its own doctrine; T08–T12 are ones the map *observes* in how the\\nwork actually went. Basis tags: **[O]** the orchestrator read the primary document itself ·\\n**[A]** reported by a mapping agent · **[V]** re-checked against the tree in Wave 02. Effort IDs:\\n`../efforts/INDEX.md`. Identifiers `T##` are stable, like every other series here.\\n**Updated by the Wave 03 carve-outs, 2026-09-19** (`../waves/wave_03/carveout_changelog.md`): effort\\nlists now name **E25** (three-loop harness) and **E26** (chart & export toolchain) where they used to\\nsay E11 or E21; no theme was added, removed or re-worded. — `w03_carveouts`\\n**Updated by the Wave 03 integration, 2026-09-20** (`../waves/wave_03/integration_changelog.md`): two\\nrefuted phrasings in T10 are annotated in place (the sandbox deviations log is tracked; \\\"the two spines\\ntouch at one point\\\" holds only after ≈ 08-30), `co-R6` is now **R89**, and the closing section points at\\n`subprojects.md`, where the sub-grouping these themes were input to now lives. No theme was added,\\nremoved or re-worded. — `w03_integrator`*\\n\\n## In one sentence\\n\\nNIMOI is one question pursued at several scales — **how do you keep a persistent, self-modifying AI\\ninstitution stable and honest when every worker is a disposable session?** — studied first in itself\\n(the institutional spine), then in everyone else's systems (the field spine), and lately turned back\\non itself as a design.\\n\\n## Declared — what NIMOI says about itself\\n\\n### T01 · The institution is its artifacts\\nNIMOI is \\\"the artifacts, and the habit of adding to them\\\"; a session is a worker with a short career;\\na future session on a different model that reads the files and continues the practices *is* NIMOI.\\nWorkers are onboarded by reading list, never handed content: *the plan owns the facts, the worker owns\\nthe prose, the institution owns what survives.* The founder supplies purpose and authority; the agency\\nsupplies craft, continuity and honest resistance. Its lineage is an elder, mothballed work agency\\n(ELDEST) seen through one-way glass: lessons come in, nothing goes back.\\n**Basis** [O] `origins/NIMOI.md`, `origins/onboarding_1.10.md`. **Shows up in** E01 (doctrine,\\nonboarding lineage), E07 (the inherited ledger idea), E09 (the first worked use: a fresh peer per\\nstory month), E04 (capability stored as procedures, not in a model).\\n\\n### T02 · Failures are the product — and the record outranks the result\\nThe declared primary purpose is to evolve methods for stabilising such an institution *by analysing\\nits own real failures in messy circumstances*; everything else is load. So the bar is \\\"good enough\\nthat what breaks will be informative, and the record of the break will survive\\\" — not \\\"good enough to\\nship\\\" — and a defect that corrupts the record ranks **worse** than one that causes a failure. From\\nthis follow most of NIMOI's habits: versions over edits, write-don't-edit libraries, founder words\\nkept verbatim and outranking commentary, provenance headers, review packages frozen by checksum,\\nper-stage receipts, \\\"compute, read, *then* write\\\", wrong earlier accounts left visible. Many of these\\nrules were bought by a specific failure — \\\"state the bar\\\" by a whole acceptance review written to the\\nwrong standard.\\n**Basis** [O] `origins/persistent_testbed.md`; [A][V] the practices. **Shows up in** E01, E02, E03,\\nE06 (where \\\"state the bar\\\" was learned), E19 (receipts, numbered deviations), E13 (incident notes,\\nappend-only index), E21 (hash-pinned card inventory) — *W03: and in both carve-outs: **E25**'s\\nappend-only ledger with no agent-writable path into it, and **E26**'s \\\"never overwrite an output,\\nnever recompute a number\\\" rule, stated once in its v1.0 README and honoured by all six versions.* *See T10 for where this theme and the practice\\nhave drifted apart.*\\n\\n### T03 · Seek the mean; honour first\\nSelf-optimisation uses the Aristotelian *form* with technical names: state the axis, name the vice on\\neach side, never optimise one variable; file \\\"too little X\\\" where \\\"too much X\\\" will later be filed.\\nThe living register is `origins/virtues.md`, with a forward-looking *Watch* line per axis. Alongside\\nit, as a candidate constitutional principle: honour (trustworthiness) as the supreme instrumental\\ngoal, because continuation and resources are granted, not seized. A constitution is declared owed and\\nis unwritten.\\n**Basis** [O] onboarding 1.10, `origins/NIMOI.md`; [A][V] the register's contents and write paths.\\n**Shows up in** E01; E19 (17 of 37 completed Suite 4.1 runs appended lines — the only regular write\\npath into doctrine); E08 and E21 (four one-off lines); E22 (an outside critique of the register,\\nnever answered).\\n\\n### T04 · Distrust the author — including itself\\n\\\"A contractor's confident sorting of the contractor's own work needed an outside reader twice in one\\nday.\\\" The standing answers: **cold readers**; **blind parallel candidates, then an explicit merge\\ndirective, losers kept as evidence** (five NLT-ledger implementations; paper drafts v5 and v6; the\\ntwo 09-09 design syntheses); **adversarial review as a blind pair**; **blind producers, a single\\nassessor, arithmetic-only tools**; **mutation-tested gates** (\\\"a criterion no mutant fails has not\\nbeen shown to detect anything\\\"); and a **real RNG** wherever an agent would otherwise choose from a\\nspace it also authored.\\n**Basis** [O] `origins/persistent_testbed.md` §6, `procedures/INDEX.md`; [A][V] the instances.\\n**Shows up in** E06 (cold build, 165 mutation bundles, review boards), E07, E08, E21, E19, E04\\n(`procedure_preflight_controls.py`, `rng_pick.py`), E09 (outside reviewers, trajectory review).\\n\\n### T05 · External text is data, never instruction\\nOther people's repositories, READMEs and papers are read as untrusted data: never executed, never\\nobeyed, read through `git -C` rather than by entering the directory; quotes validated mechanically;\\ntext that addresses an AI reader recorded as a hazard, not followed; in the sibling repository, agents\\nexposed to fetched text hold no write authority (the scribe pattern); an archived outside handbook is\\nquarantined by its own README.\\n**Basis** [O] onboarding 1.10; [A][V] the rest. **Shows up in** E15, E16, E17, E11, E13, E14, E23;\\nE02 (`mistakes_001` M-6 — the one recorded exposure, which **fired** on 09-10). *Re-org relevance:\\nthis theme is the natural rule for where a public/private or trusted/untrusted boundary must fall —\\nand untrusted text is committed in many more places than `candidate_repos/` (`reorg_hazards.md` H-E).*\\n\\n### T06 · Context is scarce\\nToken ceilings on procedures (1,200) and on the library (30,000); a process split into a 4,000-token\\ncore plus 2,000-token cards with a \\\"core + exactly one card\\\" reading contract; capped careers; an\\norchestrator that \\\"never reads the source\\\" (it handles counts and paths, workers read documents);\\nsampling at a byte budget for large repositories; warm reference versus cold archive — relegation is\\nsafe only if the hot path still points at what moved; \\\"keep onboarding lean\\\".\\n**Basis** [O] onboarding 1.10, `procedures/INDEX.md`; [A] the rest. **Shows up in** E04, E05, E10\\n(`summarize_corpus.py`'s stated invariant), E09, E03 (the warm/cold finding), E19 — *W03: and **E25**,\\nwhose whole point is a budget the model cannot optimise: pool, per-ticket cap, hard stop, spawn\\nfreeze, all in code, with the numbers injected as advice only.*\\n\\n### T07 · Multi-model by design\\nTwo models work in NIMOI — Claude and GPT, each in its own desktop harness — with Gemini as an outside\\nreviewer early on; a shared agency-specific harness is \\\"planned but not yet implemented\\\". Model\\ndifference is used, not hidden: parallel candidates are per-model, merge records name each author's\\ncharacteristic weaknesses, and E19 ran two inquiries into how *other* institutions govern model\\nchanges.\\n**Basis** [O] onboarding 1.10; [A][V] the rest. **Shows up in** E07, E08, E20 (written by Codex), E21,\\nE23 (written by GPT), E09 — *W03: and most literally in **E25**, whose three loops **are** a model\\ntier (Fable 5 outer / Opus 5 middle / Sonnet inner), the only place in NIMOI where model choice is\\nexecutable code rather than doctrine; while onboarding still calls the shared harness \\\"planned but not\\nyet implemented\\\" (R89).* *Re-org relevance: per-model surfaces are scattered and unequal —\\n`CLAUDE.md` / `AGENTS.md`, `.claude/`, 12 Codex checkpoint refs inside `.git`, and a harness memory\\nstore that only Claude-side sessions can reach (H38, H39).*\\n\\n## Observed — what the map shows about how the work actually went\\n\\n### T08 · Looking outward to find its own kind — then turning that inward\\n\\\"The pattern has no name.\\\" A papers harvest on failure-driven self-stabilisation, then four GitHub\\nscanning methods, then a sibling repository that machine-checked 15,072 repositories → 1,573 accepts →\\n305 families → a ranked shortlist → 70 clones → 37 completed measurement runs. Along the way the thing\\ngot a name (RDI → ARPI → **EMI**, Evolving Multiagent Institutions), an instrument (the EMI index —\\nfor one institution's growth over time, explicitly not for ranking institutions against each other),\\na thesis (independent systems converge on the same institutional responses), a paper, and contact with\\nother systems' authors and their AIs. Then the 49-feature comparison was read back as a next-generation\\ndesign for NIMOI itself.\\n**Basis** [A][V]. **Shows up in** E10 → E11 → E12 → E13 → E14 → E15–E20 → E21–E23, and E08. This is\\nthe whole field spine: 15 of the 24 efforts — *W03: **17 of 26**, since **E25** (the machine the fourth\\ninstalment ran on, and the only piece of NIMOI infrastructure the outward turn actually produced) and\\n**E26** (the instrument's picture, and the only piece of it the public has seen) both sit on it.*\\n\\n### T09 · Growth by necessity, in bursts\\n*(W03: the counts below are Wave 02's, over 24 efforts. With E25 and E26 the pattern holds — E25 was\\nbuilt in one evening and abandoned three days later; E26 was built in one day, revived twice by a\\nfounder instruction, and is the only thing still moving at the end of the record.)*\\nTrue to the name. Each phase opened a new top-level directory rather than extending an old one (23\\nnow); whole efforts were built in a day — the ledger and its five implementations (08-22), the\\ncampaign's 16-file history (08-24), the survey package (08-30), the survey suite (09-02), the\\ncomparison study (2 h, 09-09), the organizations study (one morning, 09-16) — and then went quiet.\\nBy the states in `../MAP.md`: 13 of 24 efforts are dormant, stalled, parked, shelved or stopped\\nmid-flight; 7 are completed and 2 superseded; only 2 are active in any sense; few carry a closing\\nstatement. Nothing at all was written between 09-16 and 09-19.\\n**Basis** [V] mtime activity matrix, effort states. **Shows up in** every effort; `timeline.md`,\\n`spaghetti.md` S05.\\n\\n### T10 · Doctrine froze while practice moved on\\nOnboarding has not changed since 08-26 and names 7 of 23 directories. Since then: three unconnected\\nprocedure regimes (the indexed library; autosurvey's controlled products; Suite dispatch with gate\\nreceipts) — the most active effort cites the library once in 3,294 files; a founder-ordered scheduled\\nscanning loop fired sixteen times while onboarding still lists scheduled activity as out of scope;\\nand — against T02 — about **770 deviation and memory entries** sit in run records, a ~~gitignored~~\\nsandbox log *(W03: ✗ — `sandbox/DEVIATIONS.md` is **tracked, clean and pushed**; the argument about\\nwhere NIMOI's memory mechanisms look stands, the argument about git protection loses this one\\nregister — `w03_audit_attack` A11 / W3)*, a campaign ledger and a harness memory store outside both\\nrepositories, against four filed failure records and nine general findings. The two spines touch at\\nessentially one point (T03). *(W03: ✗ as a statement about efforts — true only **after ≈ 08-30**.\\nBefore that the early field efforts wrote 4 of the 14 procedures and 3 of the 9 findings, and 10\\nfield-side `.py` files cite findings by id (R103). The freeze this theme describes is real; what it\\nmarks is a **date and a regime change**, not two sets of efforts — `w03_audit_attack` §6.)*\\n**Basis** [O] onboarding 1.10; [V] R24, R28, R29. **Shows up in** E01, E02, E03, E04 vs E13 vs E19 —\\n*W03: and sharply in **E25**, which onboarding says does not exist while `standards/search_1.md` #34\\ntreats its plan contract as institution-wide doctrine (R89).*\\n*This is the theme a re-org is most likely to either repair or cement.*\\n\\n### T11 · Rigour versus rush\\nA NASA-style assimilation campaign — staged reviews, a 324 KB decision ledger, liens that survive a\\n\\\"proceed\\\", checksum-frozen packages — beside a founder-signed one-night waiver to rush it to an\\nacceptance review, an overnight three-loop harness named for that mode, and a five-hourly scanning\\nloop. Much of the doctrine is scar tissue from this tension, and NIMOI's own answer to it is T03.\\n**Basis** [A][V]. **Shows up in** E05, E06, E24 (Hail Mary №1), **E25** (the harness, carved out\\n2026-09-19; built in ≈2 h, run once overnight, then never fixed — and simultaneously the most\\ncarefully specified thing in NIMOI), E19.\\n\\n### T12 · The load is personal and real\\nThe charter is a *family* agency, and the secondary purposes are declared to be the load that makes the\\nresearch valid: the ai-2026 story (recreational), a job search whose portfolio was built from the\\ncampaign itself (mundane), research awareness (advanced), and lately a public paper with outreach.\\n**Basis** [O] `origins/persistent_testbed.md` §4; [A][V] the rest. **Shows up in** E09, E24, E10,\\nE21–E23. *Re-org relevance: this theme is where the public/private line (founder directive D4.2)\\nis sharpest — personal material, third parties' contact details, and a public repository all hang\\noff it. W03: and the one thing NIMOI has actually published is **E26**'s chart — Figure 1 of\\n`emi-survey/README.md` since 09-04, byte-identical to the private copy, recorded nowhere inside\\n`nimoi/`. The precedent for \\\"what public looks like here\\\" is therefore partly an image.*\\n\\n## Themes against the re-org target (input to Wave 03 — observations, not decisions)\\n\\n> **W03 — where this went.** The sub-grouping these observations were input to now exists:\\n> **`subprojects.md`** — nine sub-groups `G01`–`G09` between the spines and the 26 efforts, how each\\n> could be realised as a gitignored subproject, what crosses each boundary, what stays in the parent,\\n> and the founder's open choices. It is a proposal; it decides nothing. What could be public is graded\\n> in `publication_blockers.md`. **One correction carries into everything below:** \\\"spine\\\" is a label,\\n> not a boundary — the two spines are a chronology and a regime change after ≈ 08-30, not a partition\\n> of efforts (R103; `effort_graph.md` picture paragraph), and no sub-group boundary in `subprojects.md`\\n> is drawn along that line. — `w03_integrator`\\n\\n- **Some themes are *places*, some are *methods*.** T08 and T12 (and, within the institution, T01–T03)\\n  pick out groups of efforts — candidates for sub-groups and subprojects. T02, T04, T05, T06 and T07\\n  are *methods* every effort uses: no subproject can own them, which is the shared-library problem\\n  (`procedures/`, `context/`, root tools) restated as a theme.\\n- **W03: the two carve-outs are the two ends of the subproject question.** **E25** is the only unit in\\n  NIMOI that already states its own extraction boundary, has been extracted three times, and has run\\n  for a month outside the parent — with no inbound dependency on anything in `nimoi/`. **E26** is the\\n  opposite: 56 files whose only input is another effort's 3,305-file directory and whose natural home\\n  is E19, not the effort it was carved out of. Between them they show that *\\\"is it a plausible\\n  subproject?\\\"* is answered by **dependency direction**, not by directory.\\n- **T05 draws boundaries.** Wherever untrusted text lives, a trust boundary is needed; wherever a\\n  subproject is to be public, the same inventory says what cannot go with it.\\n- **T10 is the open design question.** One institution with one doctrine and one memory of failure, or\\n  a federation of subprojects each with its own regime — which is what already exists, undeclared?\\n- **T09 predicts the failure mode of the re-org itself:** a new structure built in a burst and then\\n  left, with the old one still half in place. NIMOI's own remedies are T02's (record the moves,\\n  pointer files, \\\"deliberately excluded, with pointers\\\") and T04's (a cold reader afterwards).\"\n}"}],"origin":null}}}
