{"ts":"2026-09-29T17:19:39.247Z","author":"harness:claude-anthropic-harness/task-7-project","name":"log/20260929T171602Z/000317","body":{"kind":"message","agent":"owner.1","turn":1,"direction":"to_agent","message":{"_type":"UserMessage","content":[{"_type":"ToolResultBlock","tool_use_id":"toolu_019bZeQFiSVwHKUSjyC4Be3H","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n \"path\": \"/bootstrap-ledger/notebook.md\",\n \"start_line\": 1,\n \"end_line\": 61,\n \"total_lines\": 61,\n \"next_offset\": null,\n \"line_truncated\": false,\n \"sha256\": \"8fb3231eda511c0ef64af115283cad08cab041e0e75073bac1aa028b120d678e\",\n \"text\": \"# bootstrap-ledger — notebook\\n\\nThis is the entry point for any agent working in `bootstrap-ledger/`. Read it first.\\n\\n## How to use this notebook\\n\\n- **Read first, and update as you go.** A successor starts from this file alone. Update it while you work, not only at the end, because a session can stop at any time.\\n- **Keep only what a successor needs to act:** what the directory is for, its current state, the work to go, and pointers. Keep it under about 100 lines.\\n- **Put detail in `mem/`.** Move records, logs, rationale and working ideas there, and leave a one-line pointer under **Pointers**.\\n  - Use one file per topic, named for the topic.\\n  - Quote founder words verbatim and mark them as the founder's.\\n  - `mem/` is free space: write there whenever it helps.\\n- **Edit the notebook and `mem/` in place.** They are working documents, so rewrite stale lines rather than appending corrections. Specs and standards are versioned instead (see **Conventions**).\\n- **One agent works here at a time.** If files change under you unexpectedly, stop and tell the human.\\n- **These are conventions, not rules.** This directory has no immutable rules file; the founder judged its work less risky than the harness and API work (2026-09-23). Improve these instructions when they stop serving, and tell the human what you changed.\\n\\n## What this directory is\\n\\nThe ledger work for the bootstrap harnesses: the wiki ledger standard, and a Python module that harnesses will import to write their own project's ledger and to read peers' ledgers live. The main `nimoi` repository tracks this directory. `bootstrap-harness/` is a separate repository.\\n\\n- `standard/`: the wiki ledger standard, one file per version.\\n- `python-scribe/`: the spec (one file per version), the module `scribe.py`, its `tests/` and `interfaces.md`.\\n- `mem/`: records (see above).\\n- `reader/`: local, read-only web interface built on `scribe.load()`; see its README.\\n\\n## Conventions\\n\\n- **Bar:** good enough that what breaks is informative and the record of the break survives. Not production. Corrupting the record is worse than failing (spec §1).\\n- **Versions:** a new version of the standard or the spec is a new file, and the highest version number is the one in force. Edit a version in place only if you created it in your own session and nothing depends on it yet.\\n- **The scribe always implements the latest standard** and names that version in headers (founder, 2026-09-23). A new standard version therefore means updating the module; a test enforces this.\\n- **Module:** Python standard library only; `unittest` tests using temporary directories.\\n\\n## Current state (2026-09-24)\\n\\n- **Reader layout completed (2026-09-25):** compact metadata/body rows and a persistent sidebar with filters, nearby ledgers and entry navigation. Filters survive detail/history/local-link navigation; optional ledger-order sorting. Nine reader tests pass and browser layout/navigation checked [checked: unittest discovery and browser, 2026-09-25]. Bar: understandable read-only records with visible failures; see `mem/reader.md`.\\n- **Reader update completed (2026-09-25):** table rows show three-line body previews with in-place expand/collapse. Seven reader tests pass; browser expansion and collapse checked on disposable text/JSON sample, and the user's ledger tab refreshed. Bar: a small read-only UI change with informative failures and preserved ledger records; see `mem/reader.md`.\\n- **Reader: completed.** `reader/server.py` serves a read-only local browser with filesystem selection, combined tag/name/current-body-timestamp filters, history and wiki links. Run `python bootstrap-ledger/reader/server.py --open`; see `reader/README.md`. Seven reader tests and 44 scribe tests pass [checked: unittest discovery, 2026-09-24]; browser flow checked with disposable sample data. Bar: informative failures and preserved ledger records. Record and limitations: `mem/reader.md`.\\n- **Standard:** `standard/wiki_ledger_v0.4.md` is the latest version, a pre-1.0 draft in use.\\n  - Its §17 corrects what v0.3 promised about live readers, after a peer review; §16 covers v0.3's live reading and ownership by convention.\\n  - The format has not changed since v0.2. v0.1–v0.3 are kept as history, v0.3 as the version reviewed.\\n- **Spec:** `python-scribe/spec_v0.3.md` is the contract, including the founder's decisions (§9) and the peer-review resolutions (§10). v0.1 and v0.2 are kept as history.\\n- **Module:** `python-scribe/scribe.py` implements spec v0.3 and awaits founder review. No harness uses it yet.\\n  - Tests: `python -m unittest discover -s bootstrap-ledger/python-scribe/tests -v` (44 pass, about 7 seconds).\\n  - Negative control: `python bootstrap-ledger/python-scribe/tests/mutants.py` (26 mutants, 25 caught; the no-`fsync` mutant survives by design). Earlier counts written here were one too high; see the correction in `mem/ledger-record.md`.\\n- **Interfaces:** `python-scribe/interfaces.md` is the summary for harness authors, including peers that read live.\\n- **Architecture** (founder, 2026-09-24): each project has one ledger, owned by one harness or scribe at a time. Other projects read it, live if needed, and never write it. This is a convention for now.\\n\\n## Work to go\\n\\n1. **Founder review of the module.** Any behaviour change goes into the spec as well as the code, and `mutants.py` patterns must still match.\\n2. **Set up git before the first ledger is committed.** The repository holding a ledger root needs `*.ledger -text` in `.gitattributes`, and must ignore `lease.json` (W§12, spec §3). Neither repository has a `.gitattributes` today. This is bootstrap-harness work.\\n3. **Import mechanics across the two repositories:** bootstrap-harness work.\\n4. **Peer discovery:** how a harness finds other projects' ledger paths. The standard expects a registry (W§10); a per-harness config would do for now.\\n5. **Adopting a new standard version** means updating `LEDGER_VERSION`, `README` and `KNOWN_VERSIONS`. The version test fails until that is done.\\n6. **Standard open items (W§14):** checkpoints, batch append, ownership enforcement and others. None of them blocks bootstrapping.\\n7. **An unguarded gap on POSIX:** a process that forks after opening a scribe hands the child a writer without the lock. It cannot happen with Windows' default process start, and there is no guard; a process-id check would be small.\\n\\n## Pointers\\n\\n- `mem/ledger-record.md`: how the standard, the spec and the module were made. It covers the evaluation, how the founder's decisions were applied, implementation notes, the lease defect and live reading, and judgment calls not yet confirmed.\\n- `python-scribe/interfaces.md`: what the module offers and what a harness must do.\"\n}"}],"is_error":null}],"uuid":"f94d4d50-1751-4900-8084-2137671a0445","parent_tool_use_id":null,"tool_use_result":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n \"path\": \"/bootstrap-ledger/notebook.md\",\n \"start_line\": 1,\n \"end_line\": 61,\n \"total_lines\": 61,\n \"next_offset\": null,\n \"line_truncated\": false,\n \"sha256\": \"8fb3231eda511c0ef64af115283cad08cab041e0e75073bac1aa028b120d678e\",\n \"text\": \"# bootstrap-ledger — notebook\\n\\nThis is the entry point for any agent working in `bootstrap-ledger/`. Read it first.\\n\\n## How to use this notebook\\n\\n- **Read first, and update as you go.** A successor starts from this file alone. Update it while you work, not only at the end, because a session can stop at any time.\\n- **Keep only what a successor needs to act:** what the directory is for, its current state, the work to go, and pointers. Keep it under about 100 lines.\\n- **Put detail in `mem/`.** Move records, logs, rationale and working ideas there, and leave a one-line pointer under **Pointers**.\\n  - Use one file per topic, named for the topic.\\n  - Quote founder words verbatim and mark them as the founder's.\\n  - `mem/` is free space: write there whenever it helps.\\n- **Edit the notebook and `mem/` in place.** They are working documents, so rewrite stale lines rather than appending corrections. Specs and standards are versioned instead (see **Conventions**).\\n- **One agent works here at a time.** If files change under you unexpectedly, stop and tell the human.\\n- **These are conventions, not rules.** This directory has no immutable rules file; the founder judged its work less risky than the harness and API work (2026-09-23). Improve these instructions when they stop serving, and tell the human what you changed.\\n\\n## What this directory is\\n\\nThe ledger work for the bootstrap harnesses: the wiki ledger standard, and a Python module that harnesses will import to write their own project's ledger and to read peers' ledgers live. The main `nimoi` repository tracks this directory. `bootstrap-harness/` is a separate repository.\\n\\n- `standard/`: the wiki ledger standard, one file per version.\\n- `python-scribe/`: the spec (one file per version), the module `scribe.py`, its `tests/` and `interfaces.md`.\\n- `mem/`: records (see above).\\n- `reader/`: local, read-only web interface built on `scribe.load()`; see its README.\\n\\n## Conventions\\n\\n- **Bar:** good enough that what breaks is informative and the record of the break survives. Not production. Corrupting the record