{"ts":"2026-09-29T17:20:34.323Z","author":"harness:claude-anthropic-harness/task-7-project","name":"log/20260929T171602Z/000562","body":{"kind":"codex_rpc","agent":"owner.2","turn":1,"direction":"from_codex","rpc":"rawResponseItem/completed","rpc_id":null,"message":{"method":"rawResponseItem/completed","params":{"threadId":"01a0ee2d-9fad-7f31-a203-ab008d6683f2","turnId":"01a0ee2d-a1db-7190-8b90-ba922ad2767b","item":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a0ee2e-b812-7af2-9832-c5d731178ea1","call_id":"call_nxJLfWSg47iLGGdkrayNZ5uF","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.1 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"### /procedures/failure_record.md 1-59"},{"type":"input_text","text":"# Subprocedure: Failure Record\n\nVersion: 1 · Supersedes: — · Effective-Date: 2026-08-26 · State: directed\nOrigin: founder direction, 2026-08-26, after a read of six external repositories in\n`candidate_repos/`. **The format is adapted from an external source** — the bug reports in\n`maci0/clanker` under `docs/reports/bugs/`, examined in\n`context/candidate_repos_areev_clanker_read_2026-08-26.md`. It was rewritten here rather than\ncopied; no span crosses from that repository into this file. The provenance is stated because\n`processes/procedure_deployment_entry.md` §2 makes undeclared external text the first thing a\ntriggered audit looks for.\n\n**Why it exists.** Failures are the agency's product, and an unrecorded failure is a failure\nbought and thrown away. This procedure is for writing the record.\n\n## When to use\n\nA failure the agency wants back later: a defect in tooling or a procedure, a run that produced a\nwrong result, a mechanism that silently stopped working. **Priority goes to failures that corrupt\nthe record** — a stale ledger, a lost history, an instrument reporting success it did not have —\nbecause those cost more than failures that merely break something.\n\nNot for: a finding true outside the work that produced it (that is `general_findings.md`), or a\none-line mistake with no mechanism behind it.\n\n## Format\n\nOne failure per file, `<area>/reports/<YYYY-MM-DD>-<slug>.md`, dated the day the failure was\n*established*, not the day it was written. Sections, in this order:\n\n- **TL;DR** — what failed · what it cost · resolved or open.\n- **Symptom** — including *no symptom*, when the failure was silent. Silence is the finding.\n- **Reproduction** — every claim carries the command that produced it and that command's **actual\n  output**, with the date and the tree it was run in. A claim without a command is marked\n  **unverified** and stays visible as such.\n- **Root cause** — the mechanism, not the area. Where a **control** exists — something that\n  survived the same conditions — quote it; it is what separates the mechanism from its neighbours.\n- **What was lost** — counted. If some of it is unrecoverable, say how much and say it is gone.\n- **Resolution** — recovery before fix, in that order, since a fix rarely resurrects anything.\n- **Verification** — what proves each half. Prefer a check that fails on the old code.\n- **Follow-up** — including **negative results**: what was checked and found *not* to be an\n  instance.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. **Establish the failure before writing.** Run the commands; keep the output.\n2. **Write the record.** New file; never edit one to mean something different.\n3. **Leave wrong earlier accounts visible.** When the investigation changed its mind, keep the\n   superseded account and say why it was wrong — *stated more confidently than it was checked* is\n   itself the recurring failure, and deleting the evidence of it removes the lesson.\n4. **Correct predecessors by citation.** If this record falsifies an earlier one, name it and say\n   plainly whether the truth is worse. Do not edit the earlier file.\n5. **Attribute across sessions.** Name the session that found each part. Where two disagree,\n   reconcile the difference in the open rather than choosing one silently.\n6. **Cite, don't restate.** Elsewhere, one line pointing here.\n\n## Logging\n\nOne line wherever the work was logged: the record's path, whether the failure is resolved or open,\nand what now cites it."},{"type":"input_text","text":"### /procedures/general_findings.md 1-72"},{"type":"input_text","text":"# Subprocedure: Documenting General Findings\n\nVersion: 1 · Supersedes: — · Effective-Date: 2026-08-13 · State: directed\nOrigin: author direction, 2026-08-13, during campaign `procedure_acquisition` closeout. A finding\nlearned in one piece of work is usually filed where it was learned and lost to everything else. This\nprocedure gives cross-cutting findings a home of their own, so the agency accumulates transferable\nknowledge instead of re-learning it per campaign.\n\n**Scope of this version: documentation only.** How findings get *applied* is deliberately undefined,\nand no tooling scans them. Both are future work; do not invent either here.\n\n## What belongs in `general_findings/`\n\nOne test: **would this still be true and useful in a piece of work that has nothing to do with where\nit was learned?**\n\n- **Yes → `general_findings/`.** Tooling behavior, source-handling hazards, model-behavior patterns,\n  research technique, anything about the environment the agency works in.\n- **No → leave it where it was learned.** Findings about a *process* go in that process's version\n  history or reference files; findings about a *campaign's subject matter* stay in the campaign.\n\nDuplication is the failure mode to avoid. If a finding is promoted here, the campaign or process\ndocument **cites it in one line rather than restating it** — a fact maintained in two places will\ndisagree within a version or two.\n\nPrefer under-promoting. One campaign's experience is thin evidence for a general claim; say so in the\nfinding rather than declining to write it, and let a second occurrence strengthen it.\n\n## Format\n\nOne finding per file, `general_findings/<id>.md`, markdown with YAML frontmatter. The frontmatter is\nbuilt **for** progressive-disclosure tooling that does not exist yet: it is machine-readable so a\nfuture index or lookup can be built over it without rewriting the corpus. Until then, discovery is by\nreading frontmatter blocks, which sit at the top of each file and are cheap to skim.\n\n```yaml\n---\nid: kebab-case-matching-filename\ntitle: Short declarative statement of the finding\ndescription: One line — what it is and when it applies. Third person. Under ~200 characters.\ntags: [tooling, research, verification]\nstatus: active            # active | superseded | retired\nconfidence: single-instance   # single-instance | corroborated | established\ncreated: 2026-08-13\norigin: where it was learned (campaign, session, or task)\nsupersedes: <id or omit>\n---\n```\n\nBody, in this order, short: **Finding** (what is true) · **Why it matters** (the cost of not knowing)\n· **What to do** (the operative response) · **Evidence** (what was actually observed, with its\nlimits).\n\n**No index file.** The frontmatter is the index; a hand-maintained list beside it is a second copy\nthat drifts. Revisit when the corpus passes roughly twenty findings or when lookup tooling arrives —\nwhichever comes first.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. **Apply the test above.** If it fails, stop; file it where it was learned.\n2. **Check for an existing finding on the same subject.** Strengthen it — raise `confidence`, add\n   evidence — rather than adding a near-duplicate.\n3. **Write the file.** New file per finding; never edit a finding to mean something different. A\n   finding that turns out wrong is **superseded** by a new file, with the old one's `status` set to\n   `superseded` and the new one's `supersedes` naming it. History is kept.\n4. **Cite, don't copy.** Replace the originating document's full account with a one-line citation to\n   the finding.\n\n## Logging\n\nOne line wherever the work was being logged (campaign ledger, session log): which findings were\npromoted, and what now cites them instead of restating them."},{"type":"input_text","text":"### /nimoi_mapping_project/efforts/E02_failure_records.md 36-93"},{"type":"input_text","text":{"digest":{"sha256":"02fe18c029e834c138032ca237ef7d5452b33575cf465e0c05bbaa9ba0623719","chars":5743,"preview":"### Where failures are actually recorded (the R29 thread, followed)\n\n| Register | Where | Volume | Convention | Git | In the hot path? |\n|---|---|---:|---|---|---|\n| **Run-record deviations** (E19) | `candidate_analysis/metrics/runs/*.md` → `## Deviations` | **643 entries across 60 run records** (48"}}},{"type":"input_text","text":"### /nimoi_mapping_project/relationships/subprojects.md 1-70"},{"type":"input_text","text":{"digest":{"sha256":"5b45486a018603d5555d2f295460039ea18dadffdc64dd5b326cd389ef01b0eb","chars":9806,"preview":"# Subprojects — sub-groups `G01`–`G09`, and how each could be realised on disk\n\n> 1. **What this is.** Level 2 of the map (`relationships/`; reached from `INDEX.md` and `../MAP.md` once the integrator adds the rows): the merged scheme for founder directive D4.2 / D4.3 (`../method/founder_directives."}}},{"type":"input_text","text":"### /nimoi_mapping_project/relationships/subprojects.md 277-326"},{"type":"input_text","text":{"digest":{"sha256":"fe771078b5d3b961a2cd080665d70f1251d19bf52b6957a18512ba7a017ca1db","chars":8985,"preview":"## 6 · The shared libraries (M7, M8)\n\nSix things are used across sub-group boundaries and owned by none. Themes T02, T04, T05, T06 and T07 are *methods* every effort uses, so no subproject can own them\n(`themes.md`, last section). **They are four different problems, because they differ in how they c"}}}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0ee2d-a1db-7190-8b90-ba922ad2767b","create_time":1790702434.322653}}},"emittedAtMs":1790702434322}}}
