{"ts":"2026-09-29T17:28:33.218Z","author":"harness:claude-anthropic-harness/task-7-project","name":"log/20260929T171602Z/001185","body":"[harness] Task owner owner.1 (claude-opus-5-5) finished turn 1: success; $2.53 with its subagents so far. It is idle: give it a new instruction with message_owner, or stop it with stop_owner.\nIts reply ([[log/20260929T171602Z/001177]]):\nI've written the doctrine-priorities recommendation to `work/doctrine-priorities/claude/recommendation.md` (from ledger revision `20260929T171602Z:4677`). A cold reader checked it and I fixed all five defects it found. **State: completed.** Acting on it needs founder rulings, which are not mine to make.\n\n**My bar,** stated at the top of the deliverable: for each item the founder can see what doctrine is proposed, why it has that rank, the map evidence with each claim labelled checked or working, and the smallest act that would settle it. Asking for a constitution-sized writing effort before launch would fail it.\n\n**What I decided.**\n- **What counts as doctrine:** standing rules only, following the founder's 09-24 distinction between standing directives and task instructions. Effort state calls and re-org steps are not doctrine.\n- **How I ranked:** by what governed self-modification will act on, or act without, in its first days. The criteria, in order: record integrity (NIMOI's own ranking), dependency between doctrine items, and cost of absence against cost of writing.\n- **How deep I read:** the map's two entry files, the founder directives, the themes, the E01 and E02 effort files, the 52 founder questions, the efforts index, `persistent_testbed.md`, onboarding 1.12, and the two subprojects' entry documents. That is enough to rank, not to draft doctrine.\n\n**The ranking:**\n1. **Amendment and authority:** who may change doctrine, a register of doctrine documents, and a register of which founder statements are standing.\n2. **Failure memory:** which failure-record scheme wins, whether the roughly 770 records outside the memory system count, and whether the project ledger is the failure record from launch on.\n3. **Reconcile the bar rule:** onboarding 1.12 says a worker given no bar stops and asks; the founder's rule of today says task owners set their own. They conflict now.\n4. **Procedure regime:** one regime for the institution, or a federation of subprojects stated openly.\n5. **Publication gate:** a standing ban on publication or export by agents without founder authorization for each act, until a full standard exists.\n6. **Virtue register:** its status and upkeep.\n7. **Organizational responsibility:** mapping the existing statement onto the governor, owner and subagent layers.\n8. **The constitution:** deliberately deferred until after launch.\n\nThe deliverable also has a \"founder's one hour\" shortlist, four `DISCREPANCY:` lines, two AI-addressed files recorded by path (both founder-sanctioned, neither followed), and a not-looked-at list.\n\n**Unresolved:**\n- Most map claims are working, not checked by me: I read only a sample, and every git-state claim is the map's.\n- The cold reader could not reach the `/bootstrap-harness` listing or the assignment entries, so my own reads of those stand unreviewed.\n- No personal or third-party data is quoted, so no confirmation was needed.\n\n**Cost:** about $1.5 of $5, including about $0.97 for the cold reader.\n\n### Observations on the harness and tools\n\n**Observed:**\n- The onboarding-first gate worked.\n- Bounds were enforced exactly. The cold reader reported that listing `/bootstrap-harness` was refused, because its fs.read covered only `rules.md` in that directory.\n- Spawning, pinning instructions to a revision, and the budget cap all worked. The child read onboarding and wrote its report to the ledger path I granted.\n- `fs.write` from a ledger entry whose body is a JSON string produced a 20,460-byte file.\n- The harness twice injected \"The user hasn't heard from you in a while\" prompts mid-task. The environment block names Bash and PowerShell, which are not in my tool list; I did not use them.\n- The budget display was jumpy: it showed $1.23 right after a child that cost $0.97, and the spawn result reported `session_cost_usd` 1.31.\n\n**Inferred:**\n- The 20,460-byte file is probably the text body written exactly, but I did not read it back.\n- The budget display may lag or count the child separat [...clipped; read the link]"}
