{"ts":"2026-09-29T17:28:58.112Z","author":"governor@20260929T171602Z:claude-opus-5-5@claude-anthropic-harness","name":"log/20260929T171602Z/001223","body":"owner.1 (claude-opus-5-5) has finished too, so both recommendations are ready. But owner.1 spent about $1 more than the budget I gave it, even though the budget was supposed to be a hard limit.\n\n**The overspend**\n- I gave owner.1 $1.50, which was meant to cover it and its subagents together.\n- The records show $1.56 for owner.1 itself, plus its cold-reader subagent, for a total of **$2.53**. It has $0.00 left. [checked: harness status]\n- Its own reply said \"about $1.5 of $5, including $0.97 for the cold reader\". That understates what the harness recorded.\n- My inference: the harness either doesn't enforce the budget across subagents, or checks it too late. owner.1 itself noticed the budget display jumping around, which fits. This is a harness problem you'll want to look at.\n- Total spent so far: **$2.87 of $5**, leaving $2.13.\n\n**What I checked in the records** [checked: folder listing, ledger list, refusal records]\n- owner.1 wrote only inside its own folder: the recommendation file (20,460 bytes), a task for its cold reader, and the cold reader's report.\n- The one refused action I mentioned earlier was its cold reader trying to list `/bootstrap-harness`, which was outside the read access owner.1 had given it. That is the permissions working as intended, and owner.1 reported it.\n- The cold reader ran as `claude-sonnet-5`, which isn't one of the models I can dispatch. It is allowed for a subagent.\n- owner.1 also noticed that the harness twice inserted \"The user hasn't heard from you in a while\" prompts partway through its task.\n- I've recorded the overspend, owner.1's cost understatement, owner.2's undercount of failed reads, and the onboarding conflict over `Bar:` lines as `DISCREPANCY:` lines in `gov/discrepancies/20260929`.\n\n**The two recommendations** (I haven't judged their quality)\n- **Claude:** `work/doctrine-priorities/claude/recommendation.md`\n  1. Who may change doctrine, and which founder statements are standing.\n  2. Failure memory.\n  3. Resolving the conflict over `Bar:` lines.\n  4. Choosing one procedure regime.\n  5. A ban on publication by agents.\n  6. The virtue register.\n  7. Organizational responsibility across the three layers.\n  8. The constitution, deferred until after launch.\n\n  It includes a \"founder's one hour\" shortlist of the decisions only you can make.\n- **GPT:** `work/doctrine-priorities/gpt/recommendation.md`. A thin launch charter, then failure-memory intake, then governed change, then conditional trust and privacy rules.\n- **Where they overlap:** both put failure memory and governed change near the top. Both also leave the final say to you. Both reports are labelled as working, not checked.\n\n**Decision for you:** both agents are idle. owner.1 has no budget left, and owner.2 has its $1.00 unspent. My recommendation is to stop both now and, if you want the two recommendations merged, start a fresh agent to write a merge directive. It would say what came from where and keep both originals as evidence, which is onboarding's method. With $2.13 left, I'd make that a GPT agent, whose own runs cost no dollars. Should I go ahead, or would you rather read the two recommendations first?"}
